Common causes of memory leaks
=============================

Unbounded collections
==============
Keeping objects in List/Map/Set without removal (e.g., caching responses/users without TTL/eviction).
Keys with poor cardinality causing ever-growing maps.



Static references & singletons
==============================
static fields holding large graphs (e.g., caches, DTOs).
Singletons retaining references to request/session objects.



Listeners/Callbacks not removed
==============================
Event listeners/observers (e.g., GUI, RxJava/Reactor) registered but never deregistered/disposed.



ThreadLocal misuse
=================
Not clearing ThreadLocal in thread pools (app servers reuse threads → data sticks around).
Large objects stored in ThreadLocal (e.g., SimpleDateFormat, byte buffers).

Not closing resources (I/O & DB)
===============================
Missing close() on InputStream, OutputStream, ResultSet, Statement, Connection, ZipFile, HttpClient responses, etc.
Not using try-with-resources.



Leaking threads / executors
==========================
Creating threads/timers that are never stopped.
ExecutorService not shutdown() → threads + task queues retain objects.



ClassLoader leaks (app servers)
==========================
Static singletons stored in libraries referencing app classes.
JDBC drivers, logging frameworks, or Timer tasks loaded by app ClassLoader and never deregistered on redeploy.
ThreadLocal values referencing webapp classes.



Improper caching strategies
=========================
Caches without eviction/size limits.
Using strong references instead of WeakReference/SoftReference where appropriate.



Long-lived sessions/scopes
=============================
Storing heavy objects in HTTP session or Spring singleton scope (e.g., request data, file bytes).



Retaining large temporary data
============================
Accumulating logs/metrics/batch records in memory prior to flush.
Holding entire files in memory instead of streaming.


Off-heap/native memory leaks
============================
Direct byte buffers (ByteBuffer.allocateDirect), Netty ByteBuf not released.
JNI libraries leaking native memory.


Accidental object retention via closures
=============================
Lambdas/inner classes capturing outer large objects (e.g., capturing this when only a small field is needed).


Finalizers/cleaners misuse


Relying on finalizers/cleaners for resource cleanup (unpredictable; delays reclamation).


Mutable keys in hash-based collections


Changing fields that participate in hashCode()/equals() after insertion prevents removal → structure grows.


Logging frameworks & MDC


MDC/thread context not cleared in thread pools; holds onto user/request context indefinitely.


Quick prevention tips

Use try-with-resources for I/O/DB.
Prefer bounded caches (Caffeine/Guava) with size/TTL and proper eviction.
Always deregister listeners and dispose reactive subscriptions.
Clear ThreadLocal in finally or use frameworks/utilities that do it.
Shutdown executors and timers on application stop.
In web apps, avoid putting heavy data in session/singleton scope.
For app servers, ensure JDBC drivers/loggers are deregistered on undeploy; avoid static references to webapp classes.
For Netty/direct buffers, release buffers properly.

How to detect quickly

Take a heap dump and analyze with Eclipse MAT (Leak Suspects report, Dominator Tree).
Enable GC logs; watch for frequent Full GCs and steadily rising heap.
Use allocation/retained size views in profilers (YourKit, JProfiler, VisualVM).
Check thread dumps for runaway executors/timers.


